From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: tune the sample delay instead of running it at zero

HS200 on this controller has never been tuned. It runs on the sample delay
sunxi_mmc_calibrate() writes -- SAMP_DL_SW_EN with a delay of 0 -- and that
value was never chosen: it is the absence of a choice, the same defect `mmc-sunxi-a733-hs400-data-strobe-delay-value` and `mmc-sunxi-tune-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay`
found one mode over on the HS400 data strobe.

THE TWO HALVES OF WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE (read in 6.18.36, this series applied)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
- drivers/mmc/core/core.c, mmc_execute_tuning():

      if (!host->ops->execute_tuning)
              return 0;

  We register no ->execute_tuning, so the function the core calls to tune HS200
  succeeds without sending a single tuning block. mmc_hs200_tuning() returns its
  0, mmc_init_card() carries on, and nothing above the driver can tell a tuned
  bus from an untuned one.
- drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c, sunxi_mmc_calibrate(): writes SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN
  and nothing else, i.e. the software delay line is enabled at value 0.

WHAT THE UM SAYS ABOUT THE KNOB
-------------------------------
a733-um-v0.92, 11.3.8.37, offset 0x0144, SMHC_SAMP_DL, default 0x0000_2000:

  [13:8] SAMP_DL   R    0x20   (listed without a name in the bit table; the
                                UM's own text for SAMP_DL_SW refers to the
                                value of SAMP_DL)
  [7]    SAMP_DL_SW_EN  R/W  0x0  enables the delay set in SAMP_DL_SW
  [5:0]  SAMP_DL_SW     R/W  0x0  software sample delay: the command and
                                data lines relative to the clock; the UM
                                says it is chosen from SAMP_DL, the card
                                clock period and the device's input timing
                                requirement

So: a 6-bit software delay, a software enable, and no documented hardware
calibration engine. The UM saying the value depends on the card clock and
the device's input timing means it is a measurement, not a constant.
It was also observed on silicon that [13:8] carries runtime state and must
never be written.

WHAT THIS DOES
--------------
Registers ->execute_tuning and sweeps SAMP_DL_SW 0..63, scoring each candidate
with mmc_send_tuning(), which sends the tuning command (CMD21 for an eMMC,
CMD19 for an SD card -- mmc_execute_tuning() picks the opcode) and compares the
returned block against the JEDEC tuning pattern. A delay passes only when every
bit came back right. The midpoint of the widest passing run is programmed, in
the same shape as the HS400 strobe sweep `mmc-sunxi-tune-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay` added, and for the same reason:
the midpoint has a known distance to both edges, the first passing value does
not.

Three details that are decisions, not style:

- The window search does not wrap. The delay line is a chain, not a phase ring;
  63 does not border on 0.
- The chosen value is re-tested once after it is programmed. The sweep proves a
  value passed while the sweep was running; the confirmation proves the value
  the bus is actually left on passes.
- A sweep that finds nothing does not fail the card. An error here reaches
  mmc_init_card() through mmc_hs200_tuning() and aborts the entire init
  ("err = mmc_hs200_tuning(card); if (!err) err = mmc_select_hs400(card); ...
  if (err) goto free_card"). Failing would therefore be a regression against the
  untuned driver, which reaches HS200 on delay 0 today. It keeps delay 0, logs
  at dev_err, and returns 0.

sunxi_mmc_calibrate() now takes the ios and reprograms the tuned value. Without
that the tuned delay would live until the next set_ios: that path runs on every
clock change and again from sunxi_mmc_runtime_resume(), which calls
sunxi_mmc_set_clk() unconditionally. host->samp_dl is 0 until a sweep sets it,
so every controller and every mode that does not tune writes exactly the byte it
wrote before.

WHICH TIMINGS GET THE TUNED VALUE
----------------------------------------------
Two, and no others:

- the timing the sweep ran at (HS200 on an eMMC, SDR104 on an SD card);
- HS400, when the sweep ran in HS200 and ios->clock is the clock it ran at.

Every other set_ios writes SAMP_DL_SW_EN | 0, the byte it wrote before. That
covers the steps mmc_select_hs400() passes through on the way up -- HS, first at
the HS200 clock and then at hs_max_dtr (drivers/mmc/core/mmc.c:1212-1216), and
HS400 at that lower clock (mmc.c:1250) until mmc_set_bus_speed() raises it
(mmc.c:1251) -- and, on the way down, mmc_hs400_to_hs200() lowering the HS400
clock to hs_max_dtr and passing DDR52 and HS (mmc.c:1290, 1303, 1316). Each of
them is a set_ios and reaches sunxi_mmc_calibrate(). A gate on the timing alone
also wrote 0 at the final HS400 set_ios, so the HS200 result never reached the
mode it was measured for.

Why HS400 gets the HS200 value, line by line against 6.18.36 and the UM:

1. The mmc core never tunes in HS400; it tunes in HS200 for HS400.
   mmc_init_card() runs mmc_hs200_tuning() and only then mmc_select_hs400()
   (mmc.c:1836-1841), and mmc_hs200_tuning() first prepares "the HS400 target
   operation frequency for tuning process" (mmc.c:1601-1608). A retune from
   HS400 goes down to HS200, tunes there and switches back up (mmc_retune(),
   drivers/mmc/core/host.c:193-206). So the HS200 result is the only measured
   sample delay an HS400 bus can ever have; the alternative is the unmeasured 0.
   Mainline hosts do the same: sdhci-msm restores its HS200 tuning phase for
   HS400 because "Retuning in HS400 (DDR mode) will fail"
   (drivers/mmc/host/sdhci-msm.c:924-933).
2. HS200 and HS400 run the same card clock. mmc_set_bus_speed() gives both
   ext_csd.hs200_max_dtr (mmc.c:1006-1008), and this driver halves the module
   clock on its way to the card in both (the div = 2 branch of
   sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate(), `mmc-sunxi-hs400-module-clock-at-twice-the-card-clock` and `mmc-sunxi-hs200-module-clock-the-open-question-made-measurable`). Silicon agrees: SMHC2_CLK reads
   0x81000001, a 400 MHz module clock, in HS400 (the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-25,
   where the strobe window
   halving from 44 to 21 steps confirms the doubled card clock) and in HS200
   (the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19).
   A delay measured at one card clock is still not a value for another; the
   ios->clock test is what holds the code to that, not this paragraph.
3. The line is still in use in HS400. UM 11.3.8.37 describes SAMP_DL_SW as the
   delay of the command and data lines relative to the clock, with no
   mode excepted. In HS400 the card's strobe takes over the read data (UM
   11.3.5.2, note to Figure 11-7: start bits count at either strobe level,
   end bits only at the high level) -- through its
   own delay, SMHC_DS_DL (11.3.8.38), which `mmc-sunxi-tune-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay` sweeps. It does not take over
   the command response: the one place the UM samples a response on the strobe
   is SMHC_CQE_CTRL[8] DS_SAMP_RESP_EN, meant for CQE operation in HS400
   enhanced-strobe mode (11.3.8.41), and this driver sets that bit only in
   enhanced-strobe mode. In plain HS400 every command response is sampled
   against the clock through SAMP_DL_SW -- the line CMD21 measured in HS200.

One consequence is intended: on a plain-HS400 card, `mmc-sunxi-tune-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay`'s data-strobe sweep
(->execute_hs400_tuning, called after mmc_set_bus_speed(), mmc.c:1251-1259) now
runs on the command sample delay the bus is left on instead of 0. Each of its
candidates is a CMD8, and the CMD8 response is on this line.

Enhanced strobe is not touched. For an hs400es card mmc_init_card() takes the
other branch (mmc.c:1847-1852) and never calls mmc_execute_tuning(), so the
eMMC is not swept, host->samp_dl stays 0, and HS400ES writes SW_EN | 0 as it
did. Measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-22, with `arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-emmc-enhanced-strobe` applied: SMHC_SAMP_DL
0x00000080 at timing 10, and the one tuning line in the boot log is the SD's.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES, AND WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD
------------------------------------------------
The BSP driver behind SMHC2 (drivers/mmc/sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c, the v4p6x path its
DT binds) picks the HS400 sample delay from a per-board DT entry for the HS400
command line and, without one, from the HS200/SDR104 entry of the same table at
the same frequency (sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c:364-375, 391-398). So the vendor also
treats the HS200 value as the HS400 command value. What it carries is a table
number, not a measurement: on the 8 GiB board its HS400 bus reads SAMP_DL 0x00000080,
delay 0. Its optional in-kernel tuning (DT execute_tuning_in_kernel) goes the other way and
writes the one tuned value in every timing it sets, the identification clock
and the HS and DDR52 switch steps included (sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c:351-352).

This patch carries a value measured on the running board, and only into the
timing it was measured in and into HS400, where the card clock and the sampled
line are the ones it was measured on. Everything else stays on the 0 it ran on.

WHY THIS SWEEP NEEDS NO STATUS DRAIN
------------------------------------
`mmc-sunxi-drain-the-card-status-the-hs400-sweep-dirties` had to add sunxi_mmc_drain_card_status() after the HS400 strobe sweep,
because each failed transfer there made sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop() send a
CMD12 the card could not honour, and the resulting ILLEGAL_COMMAND surfaced as
"unexpected status 0x400900 after switch". That does not happen here.
mmc_send_tuning() builds an mrq with data.blocks = 1 and no mrq->stop, and
sunxi_mmc_needs_manual_stop() returns true only for SD_IO_RW_EXTENDED, for an
mrq->stop the core attached, or for blocks > 1 -- none of which a tuning block
is (that third test is `mmc-sunxi-no-stop-command-for-a-single-block-transfer`'s). So a failed candidate sends no stop command, the
card records nothing, and there is nothing to drain. The failures themselves are
dev_dbg (sunxi_mmc_dump_errinfo), so a sweep with 50 failing candidates does not
flood the console either.

SCOPE: THIS ALSO TUNES THE SD SLOT
----------------------------------
The ops struct is shared, so SDR104 on SMHC0 gets the same sweep with CMD19.
That is the intended reach -- the SD has the identical untuned delay 0 and the
identical register -- but it is the medium that carries root on the 8 GiB board under our
kernel, so it is the risk this patch carries and the acceptance run must cover
it. The bounded downside is the never-fail rule above: the worst case is the
delay 0 the slot runs today, plus 64 tuning commands per tuning pass.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
No constant is introduced. The register, its fields and their meaning come from
the UM register description cited above; the delay itself is measured on the board at every
init, which is the point. The core's control flow is read in
drivers/mmc/core/{core.c,mmc.c,host.c} of 6.18.36.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
- Applies at --fuzz=0 on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36 with the whole series in
  numeric order: yes, this revision (2026-09-22). Every later patch applies
  unchanged.
- Builds: drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.o, arm64, W=1, no warnings, this revision
  (native, defconfig plus the options the driver needs). The first revision was
  built by the automated build job.
- Silicon, first revision (the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19): the sweep runs on both hosts and
  prints "sample-delay window 0..63 (64 wide), using 32". Every delay passes, so
  at this clock the sweep does not discriminate (observable 1 below, the
  "pointless" outcome). On the eMMC the value then did not survive the HS400
  switch: SAMP_DL read 0x0080 on mmc2 while mmc0 (SDR104) held 0x20a0.
- Silicon, this revision: NOT proven. The observable, on a kernel without `arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-emmc-enhanced-strobe`
  so that the eMMC runs plain HS400 and is tuned in HS200 first: while the
  eMMC host's debugfs ios reads "timing spec: 10", SMHC_SAMP_DL (0x04022144)
  reads SW_EN plus the delay the HS200 sweep printed -- 0x000020a0 if it picks
  32 again, since [13:8] mirrors the applied delay.
  0x00000080 there means the value is still dropped.
- Still open from the first revision, on the 8 GiB board:
  1. A window that discriminates: "tuning: sample-delay window A..B (N wide)"
     with 0 < N < 64 on a host that reaches HS200 or SDR104. N = 64 (every
     delay passes) means the delay line does not reach far enough to matter at
     this clock, or the writes are not landing at all; N = 0 means the opposite.
     Both would make this patch pointless rather than wrong, and both are
     visible in the [passed] bitmap printed beside it.
  2. The HS200 sequential and random numbers from the same raw-region benchmark
     used in an earlier investigation, against the same run with A7S_SKIP_PATCHES set to this
     patch's name. If they are equal inside the noise, the delay was not the
     limit and this patch buys nothing.
  What remains unproven either way: whether the tuned delay is stable across
  temperature. The sweep runs once per init, and a retune only happens when the
  core asks for one.

ISSUE
-----
HS200 is half of the "correct and fast in HS400 and in HS200" target, and an
untuned sample delay is the first thing in that path that was never set. The
tuned value has to reach HS400, which the maintainer decided on 2026-09-22 to
repair here rather than drop the patch.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -452,6 +452,16 @@
 	u32		ds_dl;
 
 	/*
+	 * Sample-delay value (0..63) picked by ->execute_tuning, and the
+	 * ios->timing and ios->clock it was picked at. Zero / MMC_TIMING_LEGACY
+	 * / 0 until a sweep runs, which is what a controller that never tunes
+	 * keeps -- so the delay such a controller programs is unchanged by this.
+	 */
+	u32		samp_dl;
+	unsigned char	samp_dl_timing;
+	unsigned int	samp_dl_clock;
+
+	/*
 	 * Hardware busy wait (cfg->can_wait_busy). busy_wait is set while a
 	 * request has finished on the bus and the card still holds DAT0;
 	 * busy_seq tells busy_work which wait it was started for, because the
@@ -1316,21 +1326,65 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static int sunxi_mmc_calibrate(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host, int reg_off)
+static int sunxi_mmc_calibrate(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
+			       struct mmc_ios *ios, int reg_off)
 {
+	u32 dl = 0;
+
 	if (!host->cfg->can_calibrate)
 		return 0;
 
 	/*
 	 * FIXME:
 	 * This is not clear how the calibration is supposed to work
-	 * yet. The best rate have been obtained by simply setting the
-	 * delay to 0, as Allwinner does in its BSP.
+	 * yet. There is no hardware calibration engine documented behind
+	 * SMHC_SAMP_DL: the UM gives a software enable at [7] and a software
+	 * value at [5:0] and nothing else (11.3.8.37), so the value has to be
+	 * supplied from here. Delay 0 is what this driver has always written
+	 * when nothing was measured.
+	 *
+	 * Once ->execute_tuning has measured a window, reprogram what it found
+	 * whenever the bus is in the timing it was measured in. This path runs
+	 * again on every clock change and on every runtime resume, so without
+	 * it the tuned value would survive exactly until the next set_ios.
+	 *
+	 * HS400 gets the HS200 value too, once it runs at the card clock the
+	 * sweep ran at. That is the measured value for HS400, not a borrowed
+	 * one:
+	 *
+	 * - The core never tunes in HS400. It tunes in HS200 at the HS400
+	 *   target frequency (mmc_hs200_tuning()) and only then calls
+	 *   mmc_select_hs400(); a retune from HS400 drops to HS200, tunes
+	 *   there and switches back (mmc_retune()).
+	 * - Both modes run the same card clock: mmc_set_bus_speed() gives
+	 *   HS200 and HS400 the same ext_csd.hs200_max_dtr. The clock test
+	 *   below holds the code to that rather than assuming it.
+	 * - The line is still in use. SAMP_DL_SW sets the delay of the command
+	 *   and data lines relative to the clock (UM 11.3.8.37).
+	 *   In HS400 the card's strobe frames the read data, through its own
+	 *   delay (SMHC_DS_DL, sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable()). The UM puts the
+	 *   command response on the strobe only through SMHC_CQE_CTRL
+	 *   DS_SAMP_RESP_EN, meant for CQE in HS400 enhanced-strobe mode
+	 *   (11.3.8.41), and this driver never sets it outside enhanced-strobe
+	 *   mode. Without it the response is sampled against the clock,
+	 *   through this register, in HS400 as in HS200.
+	 *
+	 * The HS, DDR52 and legacy steps that mmc_select_hs400() and
+	 * mmc_hs400_to_hs200() pass through write delay 0 as before, and so
+	 * does HS400 until mmc_set_bus_speed() has raised its clock.
 	 *
 	 * HS400 needs a non-zero data-strobe delay; that is programmed
 	 * separately in sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(), not here.
 	 */
-	writel(SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN, host->reg_base + reg_off);
+	if (reg_off == SDXC_REG_SAMP_DL_REG && host->samp_dl &&
+	    (ios->timing == host->samp_dl_timing ||
+	     (host->samp_dl_timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS200 &&
+	      ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400 &&
+	      ios->clock == host->samp_dl_clock)))
+		dl = host->samp_dl & SDXC_CAL_DL_MASK;
+
+	writel(SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN | (dl << SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_SHIFT),
+	       host->reg_base + reg_off);
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -1609,7 +1663,7 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = sunxi_mmc_calibrate(host, SDXC_REG_SAMP_DL_REG);
+	ret = sunxi_mmc_calibrate(host, ios, SDXC_REG_SAMP_DL_REG);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
@@ -2105,12 +2159,116 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Sample-delay tuning for the single-data-rate modes (CMD21 in HS200, CMD19 on
+ * an SD card).
+ *
+ * These modes carry no strobe: the controller samples the command and data
+ * lines against its own clock, and SMHC_SAMP_DL is the only thing that moves
+ * that sampling point. A733 UM 11.3.8.37, offset 0x0144, reset 0x0000_2000:
+ * SAMP_DL_SW at [5:0] is the command and data line delay relative to the
+ * clock, enabled by SAMP_DL_SW_EN at [7]; [13:8] is read-only and
+ * the UM names no calibration engine behind it. The delay is software's job.
+ *
+ * Until now no software did it. sunxi_mmc_calibrate() writes SAMP_DL_SW_EN with
+ * delay 0 on every clock change, and the driver registered no ->execute_tuning,
+ * so mmc_execute_tuning() returned 0 without sending a single tuning block
+ * ("if (!host->ops->execute_tuning) return 0;", core/core.c) and HS200 ran on a
+ * delay of zero -- not a chosen value, the absence of one. That is the defect
+ * `mmc-sunxi-tune-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay` removed for the HS400 data strobe, one mode over.
+ *
+ * So sweep it, in the same shape as the HS400 strobe sweep: 64 candidates, each
+ * scored by a real transfer, then the middle of the widest passing run. The
+ * score is mmc_send_tuning(), which sends the tuning command and compares the
+ * returned block against the JEDEC pattern, so a delay passes only when every
+ * bit of it came back right.
+ *
+ * Three deliberate choices:
+ *
+ * - The delay line is a chain, not a phase ring: 63 does not border on 0, so
+ *   the window search does not wrap.
+ * - The chosen midpoint is re-tested once after it is programmed. The sweep
+ *   proves a value passed while it was being swept; this proves the value the
+ *   bus is left on passes at the moment it is installed.
+ * - A sweep that finds nothing does NOT fail the card. An error here reaches
+ *   mmc_init_card() through mmc_hs200_tuning() and aborts the whole init
+ *   ("err = mmc_hs200_tuning(card); ... if (err) goto free_card"), so failing
+ *   would be a regression against the untuned driver, which reaches HS200 on
+ *   delay 0 and carries root on it today. It keeps delay 0, says so at
+ *   dev_err, and returns 0: never worse than the state it replaces.
+ */
+static int sunxi_mmc_execute_tuning(struct mmc_host *mmc, u32 opcode)
+{
+	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
+	int i, run_start = -1, run_len = 0, best_start = -1, best_len = 0;
+	u64 passed = 0;
+	u32 dl;
+
+	for (i = 0; i <= SDXC_CAL_DL_MASK; i++) {
+		mmc_writel(host, REG_SAMP_DL_REG,
+			   SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN | (i << SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_SHIFT));
+
+		if (!mmc_send_tuning(mmc, opcode, NULL))
+			passed |= BIT_ULL(i);
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i <= SDXC_CAL_DL_MASK; i++) {
+		if (passed & BIT_ULL(i)) {
+			if (run_start < 0)
+				run_start = i;
+			run_len++;
+			if (run_len > best_len) {
+				best_len = run_len;
+				best_start = run_start;
+			}
+		} else {
+			run_start = -1;
+			run_len = 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (best_len) {
+		dl = best_start + best_len / 2;
+		mmc_writel(host, REG_SAMP_DL_REG,
+			   SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN |
+			   ((dl & SDXC_CAL_DL_MASK) << SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_SHIFT));
+
+		if (mmc_send_tuning(mmc, opcode, NULL)) {
+			dev_err(mmc_dev(mmc),
+				"tuning: sample delay %u failed its confirmation, staying on delay 0 [%016llx]\n",
+				dl, passed);
+			best_len = 0;
+		} else {
+			host->samp_dl = dl;
+			host->samp_dl_timing = mmc->ios.timing;
+			host->samp_dl_clock = mmc->ios.clock;
+			dev_info(mmc_dev(mmc),
+				 "tuning: sample-delay window %d..%d (%d wide), using %u [%016llx]\n",
+				 best_start, best_start + best_len - 1,
+				 best_len, dl, passed);
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (!best_len) {
+		host->samp_dl = 0;
+		host->samp_dl_timing = MMC_TIMING_LEGACY;
+		host->samp_dl_clock = 0;
+		mmc_writel(host, REG_SAMP_DL_REG, SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN);
+		dev_err(mmc_dev(mmc),
+			"tuning: no sample delay passes, staying on delay 0 [%016llx]\n",
+			passed);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static const struct mmc_host_ops sunxi_mmc_ops = {
 	.request	 = sunxi_mmc_request,
 	.set_ios	 = sunxi_mmc_set_ios,
 	.get_ro		 = mmc_gpio_get_ro,
 	.get_cd		 = mmc_gpio_get_cd,
 	.enable_sdio_irq = sunxi_mmc_enable_sdio_irq,
+	.execute_tuning	 = sunxi_mmc_execute_tuning,
 	.start_signal_voltage_switch = sunxi_mmc_volt_switch,
 	.card_hw_reset	 = sunxi_mmc_hw_reset,
 	.card_busy	 = sunxi_mmc_card_busy,
